PyTorch (ROCm/pytorch release/2.8, commit d618db8): size
embedding_backward_feature_kernel's shared-memory rows by the width it
is launched with.

WHY
The backward of torch.nn.functional.embedding (and nn.Embedding) with at
most 3072 indices and scale_grad_by_freq=False returns wrong weight
gradients on gfx803, and with some shapes reads out of bounds and kills
the process with a GPU memory access fault. Against a float64 CPU
reference the fp32 gradient is off by 2.7 (512 indices, 128 rows, dim 32)
to 26 (dim 128). 31 indices with dim 64 faults ("Memory access fault by
GPU ... Page not present", VM_CONTEXT1_PROTECTION_FAULT in the kernel
log). To reproduce: idx = torch.randint(0, 128, (512,)); w =
torch.randn(128, 64, requires_grad=True); F.embedding(idx, w).backward(
torch.randn(512, 64)); compare w.grad on the GPU and the CPU. The same
script passes on an NVIDIA T4 with the same PyTorch version, so the
kernel's logic is sound where its two warp sizes agree.

embedding_dense_backward_cuda launches the kernel with blockDim.x =
at::cuda::warp_size(), the hardware's 64 on gfx803, and sizes shared
memory as warp_size * BLOCKDIMY accumulators followed by the indices
batch. The kernel lays out its rows with C10_WARP_SIZE, which
c10/macros/Macros.h makes 32 in device code for every AMD target except
gfx9. Each 64-wide row then writes over half of the next row, and the
last row writes over the start of indices_batch, so gradients are added
into the wrong rows, and garbage row numbers index grad_weight out of
bounds.

The fix is in the kernel, not the launcher as in
gfx803-index-put-warp-size.patch: this kernel elects a leader among rows
targeting the same weight row with a ballot whose lane bits stand for
row numbers, so each threadIdx.y row must be exactly one hardware
wavefront. A 32-wide launch would put two rows in one 64-lane wavefront
and break that.

WHAT
The kernel's three shared-memory offsets use blockDim.x instead of
C10_WARP_SIZE. blockDim.x is the launcher's at::cuda::warp_size() on
every target, so CUDA and gfx9 compute the same offsets as before.

REAL-HARDWARE VERIFICATION (RX 590, this image)
F.embedding backward against a float64 CPU reference, 1 to 4096 indices
into 128 rows with embedding dims 1, 32, 64 and 65 (36 cases, both sides
of the 3072-index limit of this kernel's path): largest difference
5.1e-6, against 4.6e-6 for the same script on an NVIDIA T4. The 31-index,
dim-64 case that faulted now passes. fp16 gradients with repeated indices
are within one fp16 step of a float64 reference computed from the same
fp16 inputs. The image's conv, SD1.5 UNet, rocPRIM and top-k checks and a
20-step LoRA training run give the same results as before the patch.
